NNAAMMEE
       drill - get (debug) information out of DNS(SEC)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddrriillll [ _O_P_T_I_O_N_S ] _n_a_m_e [ _@_s_e_r_v_e_r ] [ _t_y_p_e ] [ _c_l_a_s_s ]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddrriillll  is a tool to designed to get all sorts of information out of the
       DNS. It is specificly designed to be used with DNSSEC.

       The name ddrriillll is a pun on ddiigg. With ddrriillll you should be able get  even
       more information than with ddiigg.

       If  no  arguments are given class defaults to 'IN' and type to 'A'. The
       server(s) specified in /etc/resolv.conf are used to query against.


       _n_a_m_e Ask for this name.


       _@_s_e_r_v_e_r Send to query to this server. If not specified  use  the  name‐
       servers from _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f.


       _t_y_p_e  Ask for this RR type. If type is not given on the command line it
       defaults to 'A'. Except when doing a reverse lookup when it defaults to
       'PTR'.


       _c_l_a_s_s Use this class when querying.


SSAAMMPPLLEE UUSSAAGGEE
       ddrriillll mmxx mmiieekk..nnll Show the MX records of the domain miek.nl


       ddrriillll --SS jjeellttee..nnllnneettllaabbss..nnll
              Chase  any  signatures in the jelte.nlnetlab.nl domain. This op‐
              tion is only available when ldns has been compiled with openssl-
              support.


       ddrriillll --TTDD wwwwww..eexxaammppllee..ccoomm
              Do a DNSSEC (-D) trace (-T) from the rootservers down to www.ex‐
              ample.com.  This option only works when ldns has  been  compiled
              with openssl support.


       ddrriillll --ss ddnnsskkeeyy jjeellttee..nnllnneettllaabbss..nnll
              Show the DNSKEY record(s) for jelte.nlnetlabs.nl. For each found
              DNSKEY record also print the DS record.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --DD     Enable DNSSEC in the  query.  When  querying  for  DNSSEC  types
              (DNSKEY, RRSIG, DS and NSEC) this is _n_o_t automatically enabled.


       --TT     Trace  _n_a_m_e  from  the  root  down.  When  using this option the
              @server arguments is not used.


       --SS     Chase the signature(s) of 'name' to a known key or as high up in
              the tree as possible.


       --II  _I_P_v_4 _o_r _I_P_v_6 _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
              Source  address  to  query  from.   The source address has to be
              present on an interface of the host running drill.


       --VV _l_e_v_e_l
              Be more verbose. Set level to 5 to see the actual query that  is
              sent.


       --QQ     Quiet mode, this overrules -V.


       --ff _f_i_l_e
              Read the query from a file. The query must be dumped with -w.


       --ii _f_i_l_e
              read  the  answer  from  the file instead from the network. This
              aids in debugging and can be used to check if a query on disk is
              valid.   If  the file contains binary data it is assumed to be a
              query in network order.


       --ww _f_i_l_e
              Write an answer packet to file.


       --qq _f_i_l_e
              Write the query packet to file.


       --vv     Show drill's version.


       --hh     Show a short help message.


   QQUUEERRYY OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --44     Stay on ip4. Only send queries to ip4 enabled nameservers.


       --66     Stay on ip6. Only send queries to ip6 enabled nameservers.


       --aa     Use the resolver structure's fallback mechanism if the answer is
              truncated (TC=1). If a truncated packet is received and this op‐
              tion is set, drill will first send a new query with EDNS0 buffer
              size 4096.

              If  the  EDNS0 buffer size was already set to 512+ bytes, or the
              above retry also results in a  truncated  answer,  the  resolver
              structure will fall back to TCP.


       --bb _s_i_z_e
              Use size as the buffer size in the EDNS0 pseudo RR.


       --cc _f_i_l_e
              Use  file  instead of /etc/resolv.conf for nameserver configura‐
              tion.


       --dd _d_o_m_a_i_n
              When tracing (-T), start from this domain instead of the root.


       --tt     Use TCP/IP when querying a server


       --kk _k_e_y_f_i_l_e
              Use this file to read a (trusted) key from. When this options is
              given  ddrriillll tries to validate the current answer with this key.
              No chasing is done. When ddrriillll is doing a secure trace, this key
              will  be  used  as  trust  anchor.  Can contain a DNSKEY or a DS
              record.

              Alternatively, when DNSSEC enabled tracing  (--TTDD)  or  signature
              chasing (--SS), if --kk is not specified, and a default trust anchor
              (/etc/unbound/root.key) exists and contains a valid DNSKEY or DS
              record, it will be used as the trust anchor.


       --oo _m_n_e_m_o_n_i_c
              Use  this  option to set or unset specific header bits. A bit is
              set by using the bit mnemonic in CAPITAL letters. A bit is unset
              when the mnemonic is given in lowercase. The following mnemonics
              are understood by ddrriillll:

                      QR, qr: set, unset QueRy (default: on)
                      AA, aa: set, unset Authoritative Answer (default: off)
                      TC, tc: set, unset TrunCated (default: off)
                      RD, rd: set, unset Recursion Desired (default: on)
                      CD, cd: set, unset Checking Disabled  (default: off)
                      RA, ra: set, unset Recursion Available  (default: off)
                      AD, ad: set, unset Authenticated Data (default: off)

              Thus: --oo CCDD, will enable Checking Disabled, which instructs  the
              cache to not validate the answers it gives out.


       --pp _p_o_r_t
              Use this port instead of the default of 53.


       --rr _f_i_l_e
              When tracing (-T), use file as a root servers hint file.


       --ss     When encountering a DNSKEY print the equivalent DS also.


       --uu     Use UDP when querying a server. This is the default.


       --ww _f_i_l_e
              write  the answer to a file. The file will contain a hexadecimal
              dump of the query. This can be used in conjunction with -f.


       --xx     Do a reverse lookup. The type argument is not used, it is preset
              to PTR.


       --yy _<_n_a_m_e_:_k_e_y_[_:_a_l_g_o_]_>
              specify  named  base64  tsig key, and optional an algorithm (de‐
              faults to hmac-md5.sig-alg.reg.int)


       --zz     don't randomize the nameserver list before sending queries.


EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       The exit status is 0 if the looked up answer is secure and trusted,  or
       insecure.   The  exit  status  is  not 0 if the looked up answer is un‐
       trusted or bogus, or an error occurred while performing the lookup.


FFIILLEESS
       /etc/unbound/root.key
              The file from which trusted keys are loaded when no --kk option is
              given.

